<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Türkmenbaşy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krasnovodsk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Türkmenbaşy, known until 1993 as Krasnovodsk, is a port city on the eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> in western Turkmenistan. Founded as a Russian fort in 1869, it became the western terminus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-caspian-railway">Trans-Caspian Railway</a> and the main link across the Caspian to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>. In 1918 it was held by the anti-Bolshevik Transcaspian government, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/26-baku-commissars">26 Baku Commissars</a> were shot nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turkmenbasy.jpg" alt="Türkmenbaşy" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Danny Gys · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nyyazov_in_February_1998.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
